ith a bit of the tension it was  friendly but a bit of a tension that  existed between justices Thomas and  Scalia on this subject Justice Thomas  was what often jokingly called himself a  faint-hearted originalist right because  he had a little more a little more  willingness to accept a long-standing  precedent that you know that that that  expectation interests had been built  around and and where there was at least  some kind of a discernible judicial  standard right and justice Thomas was  has from time to time been more of the  view of now if the president's wrong  would just overturn it and so there's  there's a bit of a spectrum within the  conservative movement about what role  precedent ought to play I I tend to be  on the side of thinking that precedent  that that cannot be justified by the  original meaning or by textualism is  ultra vires and basically ought to be  set aside it it harkens back to  something that Bentham said do I  normally don't like to quote but I think  he said that which precedent is that  which someone invokes which is untrue in  other words you know it or something  words of that effect and and so  I think there's something to be said for  that I mean basically if it's if it's  wrong then it needs there's a very  strong argument that it should be  eliminated I would say that there are  there are variations in in the courts  jurisprudence on this you know for  example I think that certainly a Supreme  Court decision that has no discernible  judicial standard does not deserve to be  treated with the respect that the stare  decisis would normally accorded so for  example I don't know what the undue  burden standard is in abortion  jurisprudence I have no idea what that  is and and and so that's a precedent  which I think should get zero weight  there are other precedents in the  Commerce Clause area where there are  judicial standards we can have a debate  about whether that's the right way to  read the clause the interstate commerce  power or not but their standards they're  discernible they're uniform they create  expectation interests and and you can  make an argument that in that instance  if you're gonna start wiping press it in  a way you know you probably need to do  it in a more measured fashion if at all  so I know that's sort of an ambiguous  answer I tend to fall on more of the  Thomas side of the spectrum but I think  there's a fair debate in the movement  about the proper role of stare decisis  thanks so much for your time tonight sir  I really appreciate it my question for  you is concerning the relationship  between federal law and state  enforcement of those laws and I wanted  to ask you how you feel about the  growing discrepancy and/or divide  between the federal law concerning  immigration in state enforcement of  those laws and how do you see that  adapting and evolving over the rest of  the term  presidency well that's a good question  because I mean that current debate which  is often described in you know political  or policy terms is really is is really a  really cuts to the core of our  constitutional system in a lot of ways  right I mean I I think our framers  envisioned a system where basically  there were certain very discreet  enumerated powers and Congress has  authority to regulate those areas and  states have a duty to respect  congressional authority in those in  those areas and they can't make  decisions for themselves they can't  decide to refuse to respect or enforce a  federal mandate in an area where  Congress has plenary authority or  specifically delegated authority and  obviously in areas like immigration  right where where you're dealing with  questions of naturalization and  citizenship and national security and  control of our borders those are federal  questions and and states and localities  have a duty to respect federal  prerogative in in those areas and so to  answer your question in a very practical  way  I think Jeff Sessions Attorney General  Jeff Sessions got it right right sue the  state of California and and you know you  do that because you know there's an  assertion of congressional authority  federal authority that needs to be  respected states need to be really  careful about this right because the the  allocation of national and state powers  is very delicate and I think once states  start ignoring those balance of powers  right it may it may cut in their  political favor one day but will very  much cut against them another day  you know this is reminiscent of that  that one of those closing scenes from a  man for all seasons where you know st.  Thomas more does a Roper I think talk  about cutting down all the trees and  what's left right you know you as a  state you want to be very careful about  eliminating these these structural  protections even though there might be  some short-term political gain by doing  it in this case ignoring the federal  government's mandates with respect to  immigration so I think that's the first  question always has to be what's the  congressional authority under article 1  section 8 is it there if it is states  have to basically respect it this left  to be the last question of the evening  thank you for your talk I was struck by  the word virtue in your talk and I know  virtue is taking a beating is that  required when you go to Hillsdale I  thought I had a yes you know virtue  signa good virtue this virtue that it's  it's taking a bit of a beating I was  also struck by your point about the need  for a citizenry the set of a certain  character and so I'm wondering you know  what in your judgment are some of the  best most practical most effective ways  to actually foster virtue in a citizenry  you know such two citizens understand it  love it and practice it  well for me first and foremost would be  faith and family I think an  understanding of virtue or of the good  as you would put it here can be realized  most readily read it readily through a  deep spiritual life through a  development of a friendship a true  friendship with God and so I think that  first and foremost is extremely  important couldn't say that at too many  colleges but you can say that here  secondly his family right we've you know  the concept of subsidiarity has been  with with mankind for a long long time  and there is something practically  useful about it right I mean you know we  know it works and so you you you you  want to try to inculcate values at the  most decentralized core level that you  can and you have intact families are  really really important for the  development of of a virtuous citizenry  and every major political commentator  and writer has has recognized that and  then you just sort of gradually work  your way up to the local community and  so forth but family and faith are really  the two most most important things and  it's no coincidence that in the Western  cultural tradition right as family and  faith waxed or waned  so did so did a more virtuous citizenry  and more virtuous leadership I'll end on  this note because I think this is a good  question to end on that's the great  thing about your project here you know  many of you probably did come from  intact families who cared about your  education and your rearing and many of  you obviously  have a faith of some kind or another  belief in God make an effort to develop  a deep spiritual life a friendship or  relationship with God what's great about  this institution is that it takes all of  those things that those those  foundational blocks that were laid in  the earlier parts of your life and it  allows that to flourish and build upon  it and so you're getting a great  education here for four years please you  know use it for good when you leave fine  I mean you know you'll have your jobs  and that may be to advance the free  market or to to serve people in in some  particular profession or another but  you're also all citizens and you also  all going to have duties to maybe maybe  raise may raise children who will  respect the principles you're learning  about here maybe maybe that's not your  calling maybe the calling is is is to  work within a community of other people  and inculcate these values when you get  out of here but you're deeply and you're  deeply involved in this project and the  education you've had is a tremendous  privilege because it gives you unique  ability to help to advance our country's  principles into the future so I'm very  grateful for  Hillsdale being here and especially  grateful that there are people like you  who want to populate this institution  and hopefully go out and and apply all  the principles and virtues that you've  learned about here so thank you very  much it's a pleasure for me to be here
 

